On comparing the results, it is seen that the heat absorbed by the air of flask No. 1 in the first experiment is $0 degree .61$, while that liberated in receiver No. 2 is only $0 degree .58$. The difference between these numbers is of itself sufficiently small to be attributed to some circumstance whose influence one might overlook, or even to errors of observation; but, if we consider the results given in the second and third rows, we see that the temperature changes are exactly equal to each other. I think, therefore, that I am justified in concluding that, when a given volume of air is made to pass from one receiver into another which is empty and of the same volume, the temperature changes in each receiver are the same.

